course: ai
lesson: 23
title: In the news: tricked bots, real discoveries, careful words
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Nearly 2000 years ago a volcano buried a Roman town and burned a library of scrolls to black lumps that nobody could open. It was reported that AI helped researchers spot faint ink in scans of the scrolls, so words could be read again for the first time in centuries.
explain: That is AI at its best: a patient pattern-spotter helping people do something they could not do alone.
explain: The same news pages also told of copied voices, fake books under real authors' names, wrong answers about elections, and the hidden people paid to check and label answers.
explain: And news about AI can itself exaggerate, so read past the headline and use careful words: "it was reported that", because we were not there and reports can be wrong.
fact: It was reported that AI helped researchers read words in ancient scrolls burned by a volcanic eruption nearly 2000 years ago, by spotting ink patterns in scans.
fact: It was reported that an AI beat a world champion at the board game Go, after learning by playing enormous numbers of games.
fact: Being brilliant at one game does not make an AI good at everything.
fact: It was reported that chatbots sometimes give wrong or out-of-date answers about elections; official election websites are the place to check.
fact: Headlines about AI can exaggerate in both directions, overhyping or overscaring, so it is wise to read beyond the headline.
fact: It was reported that some schools first banned chatbots and later allowed them with rules; pupils should follow their school's current rules.
fact: It was reported that actors went on strike (stopped working as a protest) partly over worries about AI copying their faces and voices.
fact: Everyone deserves control over their own face and voice.
fact: It was reported that a song made with a cloned voice of a famous singer went viral and was then removed, and people argued about whether copying a voice is fair.
fact: It was reported that AI-made books were sold online under real authors' names, harming authors and readers.
fact: It was reported that AI companies paid people to check and label answers, and that some of these workers saw upsetting material and were paid little.
fact: People help build AI, and they deserve fairness.
fact: "It was reported that" separates what we know ourselves from what we heard.
exercise: What did AI reportedly help do with the scrolls burned by a volcano? | Spot ink in scans so words could be read | Glue the burned scrolls back together | Write new scrolls to replace the old ones | Find out who started the volcano
exercise: Roughly how long ago were those scrolls burned? | Nearly 2000 years ago | About 200 years ago | Nearly 20 000 years ago | About 20 years ago
exercise: How did the AI reportedly get good enough to beat a world champion at Go? | By playing enormous numbers of games | By reading the champion's secret diary first | By cheating with a hidden helper | By changing the rules in secret
exercise: After the Go match, some people thought AI was all-powerful. What is the wiser view? | It is brilliant at Go, not at everything | It can now beat people at every task | It will soon run every country | It only won because the champion was ill
exercise: Lily hears chatbots sometimes give wrong news about elections. Where should voters check? | Official election websites | The first chatbot they find | A post with the most likes | A video from a stranger
exercise: Connor's dad says some AI headlines are exaggerated. What should Connor do? | Read beyond the headline | Believe the most exciting headline | Never read any news again | Share headlines before reading them
exercise: Some schools banned chatbots, then allowed them with rules. What should a pupil do? | Follow the school's current rules | Follow the rules from last year | Ignore the rules because they change | Make up his own rules at home
exercise: Why did actors reportedly go on strike, partly? | Worries about AI copying their faces and voices | They wanted robots to act for them | They were tired of making the same kind of films | They wanted to become AI programmers
exercise: A cloned voice of a famous singer made a viral song. What happened to it? | It was reported to be removed | It won every music prize that year | The singer recorded it again for real | It became the singer's favourite song
exercise: Why is copying a singer's voice without permission unfair? | A person's voice belongs to them | Voices cannot be copied at all | Singers don't care who uses their voice | Copied voices always sound terrible
exercise: AI-made books were reportedly sold under real authors' names. Who is harmed? | The real authors and the readers | Nobody, because books are just paper | Only the shop that sold them | Only the AI that wrote them
exercise: How can readers avoid fake books? | Buy from trusted places and check the author | Buy the cheapest book they can find on any website | Only buy books with shiny covers | Choose books with the longest titles
exercise: Sam hears that companies paid people to check and label bot answers. What was also reported? | Some workers saw upsetting things and were paid little | The workers were all robots too | The work was easy, quick and very well paid for everyone | Nobody has ever helped build AI
exercise: Lily's teacher says to use "it was reported that". Why? | We weren't there, and reports can be wrong | It is the polite way to tell a lie | It means the news is definitely true | It makes the story sound older
exercise: Which of these is AI at its best in this lesson? | Helping read ancient burned scrolls | Making books under real authors' names | Copying a singer's voice for a song | Giving wrong answers about elections
test: "AI won at Go so it's smarter than every human at everything now" Best reply? | It's brilliant at Go; that doesn't make it good at everything | Yes, Go is the hardest thing in the world, so it can do anything | Yes, so people should stop learning | No, it lost every game it played
test: What would happen if voters trusted a chatbot's out-of-date answer about where to vote? | Some might go to the wrong place or miss their chance | The chatbot would update the election for them | Nothing, chatbots always know the newest details of every election | The election would be moved to match
test: Your cousin says, "Headline says AI will END ALL JOBS NEXT WEEK, so it's true." What is wise? | Read past the headline; headlines can exaggerate | Believe it, headlines are always exact | Believe it, but only if the headline is written in capital letters | Ignore all news about AI forever
test: "my mate used the singer's voice in an AI song, its just fun innit" Fair answer? | Copying someone's voice without permission is unfair to them | It's fine, famous voices belong to everyone once they're on the radio | It's fine if the song is good | It's only wrong if it costs money
test: In a cartoon a star actor finds a film using her face that she never made. Why did real actors worry about this? | They want control over their own face and voice | They wanted much more money for each photo of their faces | They wanted to act alongside robots | They were afraid of cameras
test: Which sentence uses careful news words? | It was reported that AI helped read burned scrolls | AI read every ancient book in the world | My teacher says AI is magic | Burned scrolls can't be read, end of story
test: Who should we remember when an AI gives a polite, safe answer? | The people who checked and labelled answers | Nobody, the AI taught itself good manners all on its own | Only the famous people who own AI | The users who complained the most
test: A school changed its rules about chatbots twice this year. What does this show? | Schools are still working out what is best | Schools never think about AI | Rules about AI will never change again | Pupils should choose their own rules
test: A book with a famous author's name looks odd and full of mistakes. What might be going on? | It could be a fake made under her name | Famous authors always make mistakes | The printer changed her words | It must be her first ever book
test: Which is the best example of a headline that overscares? | "Robots will take over the world by Friday!" | "It was reported that AI helped find antibiotics" | "Schools allow chatbots with new rules" | "Study shows AI makes some mistakes"
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reported contribution of artificial intelligence to the study of carbonised ancient scrolls? | It assisted in the detection of ink within scans, enabling the text to be read | It reconstructed the scrolls physically from their ashes by means of robotic instruments | It composed new texts in place of those destroyed | It established that the scrolls contained no writing
exam: It is given that a program defeated a world champion at the game of Go. Identify the inference most consistent with the established facts. | The program excels at that game, which does not establish general superiority | The program has thereby surpassed humanity in every respect and in every field of activity | The champion must have permitted the program to win | The game of Go is consequently of no further interest
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning persons engaged to check and label the answers of chatbots. | Some reportedly encountered distressing material and received low pay | All were reportedly remunerated generously for easy work | No human beings have ever participated in such work | Such persons were reportedly replaced by the chatbots themselves
exam: Ascertain the principal purpose of the formula "it was reported that" in discourse concerning current events. | To distinguish what one knows directly from what one has heard | To indicate that the event has certainly occurred | To indicate that the event is certainly fictitious | To lend the account an appearance of antiquity
